(function(){
const A=window.NLDG_APPLY_CURRENT_EVENTS_V2;if(!A)return;

A({week:2,title:'The Authority of Scripture',summary:'Scripture is God-breathed and trustworthy for forming belief and life, but faithful use requires context, humility, and obedience rather than isolated proof-texts.',scripture:['2 Timothy 3:14–17','Psalm 119:97–105','Acts 17:10–12','Hebrews 4:12–13'],memoryVerse:'2 Timothy 3:16–17',bigIdea:'The Bible is not merely information to collect or ammunition for arguments. God uses Scripture to reveal truth, correct us, form us in holiness, and equip us for faithful living.',objectives:['Explain what Paul means when he calls Scripture God-breathed.','Distinguish biblical authority from misuse of isolated verses.','Practice reading a passage in literary and historical context.','Recognize that Scripture corrects Christians as well as culture.','Choose one concrete act of obedience flowing from the text.'],openingQuestion:'When two Christians both say “the Bible says,” how should we decide whether either one is reading the passage faithfully?',sections:[{heading:'Begin with Paul’s Setting',type:'scripture',content:'Read 2 Timothy 3:14–17. Paul writes to Timothy in a world of false teaching and pressure. He points Timothy back to the sacred writings he has known and describes Scripture as God-breathed and useful for teaching, correction, training, and preparation for good work.'},{heading:'Authority Means Scripture Can Correct Me',type:'teaching',content:'We often like biblical authority when it confirms what we already believe. Authority becomes real when the text is allowed to challenge our habits, loyalties, assumptions, and desires. If Scripture can only correct other people, we have not fully submitted to it.'},{heading:'Context Protects Us from Proof-Texting',type:'discernment',content:'A verse belongs inside a paragraph, book, historical setting, and the larger story of Scripture. Reading context does not weaken the Bible. It helps us hear what the inspired author is actually saying instead of forcing our own idea onto the text.'},{heading:'Jesus Is the Center of the Story',type:'christ',content:'Christians read the whole Bible as the story of God’s creation, covenant, redemption, and restoration fulfilled in Jesus. Not every verse mentions Jesus directly, but the whole biblical story reaches its center in Him and forms a people who follow Him.'},{heading:'The Spirit Forms a Teachable Community',type:'application',content:'Faithful interpretation includes prayer, careful study, the wisdom of the church across generations, and humility before other believers. The Holy Spirit does not make every private interpretation automatically correct; He forms a people who listen, test, learn, and obey together.'},{heading:'Live It This Week',type:'challenge',content:'Choose one passage you often quote. Read the paragraph before it and after it, identify who is speaking and why, then ask: What did this mean there, what principle carries forward, and what obedience does it require from me?'}],questions:['What purposes for Scripture are named in 2 Timothy 3:16–17?','Why can a true Bible verse still be misused?','What is the difference between trusting Scripture and assuming my interpretation is infallible?','Why should context matter if the Bible is inspired?','How can Christian community help us read more faithfully?','Where is Scripture currently correcting or training you?'],prayer:'God, thank You for Your Word. Give us reverence without laziness, conviction without pride, and humility without fear. Teach us to read carefully, listen honestly, receive correction, and obey what You show us. Form us through Scripture into people who look like Jesus. Amen.',background:[{heading:'Paul’s final charge',content:'Second Timothy presents Paul urging a younger leader to remain grounded amid false teaching, suffering, and cultural pressure. Scripture is presented as formative equipment for ministry and life.'},{heading:'God-breathed',content:'The phrase describes Scripture as originating from God. Christian traditions explain inspiration in different ways, but historic Christianity has consistently treated the biblical writings as uniquely authoritative for faith and practice.'},{heading:'The Bereans',content:'Acts 17 praises hearers who receive teaching eagerly while examining the Scriptures. Openness and testing belong together.'}],theology:['Revelation: God makes Himself known rather than leaving humanity to guess.','Inspiration: Scripture is received as God-breathed through human authors.','Illumination: the Spirit helps believers understand and respond faithfully.','Holiness: Scripture trains people in righteousness, not merely correct ideas.','Community: interpretation belongs within the worshiping, discerning people of God.'],worldview:{foundation:'God’s Word has authority over every competing human authority, including personal preference and cultural fashion.',affirm:'History, language, literary study, archaeology, and careful scholarship can help readers understand the text more accurately.',caution:'Authority can be distorted when verses are isolated, weaponized, or made to answer questions they were not addressing.',response:'Read the text carefully, test interpretations, remain teachable, and allow Scripture to produce obedience and Christlike character.'},difficultQuestions:[{question:'Does biblical authority mean every verse should be read literally?',response:'Faithful reading respects genre. Poetry, parable, apocalyptic imagery, narrative, proverb, and epistle communicate truth in different ways. Taking Scripture seriously means reading each text according to the kind of writing it is.'},{question:'What if Christians disagree about a passage?',response:'Disagreement does not make truth impossible. Compare interpretations with the text, context, the rest of Scripture, historic Christian teaching, reason, evidence, and the fruit produced. Hold clear convictions with humility where faithful believers differ.'},{question:'Can tradition or experience overrule Scripture?',response:'Tradition, reason, and experience can help us understand and apply Scripture, but in Wesleyan theology Scripture remains the primary norm by which the others are tested.'}],misunderstandings:['Biblical authority means my preferred translation or interpretation can never be questioned.','Reading context weakens simple faith.','Every biblical command applies in exactly the same way to every person in every setting.','Knowing more Bible automatically equals spiritual maturity.'],ministryApplications:{personal:['Read whole paragraphs rather than isolated verses.','Keep a note of interpretations you need to revisit.'],family:['Ask children what a passage says before asking what it means to them.','Model the words “I may have misunderstood that verse.”'],church:['Teach context and genre alongside application.','Avoid using Scripture to shame questions or silence legitimate concerns.'],community:['Represent biblical claims accurately in public conversation.','Correct a misquotation or misuse without humiliating the person who shared it.']}});

A({week:3,title:'Identity in Christ vs. Cultural Identity',summary:'Culture, family, race, nationality, personality, vocation, and experience shape us, but none of them can carry the final weight of identity that belongs to Christ.',scripture:['Galatians 2:19–20','Colossians 3:1–11','Genesis 1:26–27','Philippians 3:7–11'],memoryVerse:'Galatians 2:20',bigIdea:'Following Jesus does not erase every human identity; it places every identity under His lordship. In Christ we receive a deeper belonging that corrects pride, shame, exclusion, and the need to build our worth from labels.',objectives:['Name healthy and unhealthy ways identity can shape us.','Explain identity in Christ as grace and belonging rather than self-invention.','Recognize cultural identities as meaningful but not ultimate.','Reject both shame and superiority based on labels.','Practice one way of living from belonging to Christ this week.'],openingQuestion:'Which labels help people understand you, and which labels become dangerous when they are asked to tell your whole story?',sections:[{heading:'Begin with Union with Christ',type:'scripture',content:'Read Galatians 2:19–20. Paul describes a life reordered by participation in Christ. His old sources of status and righteousness no longer have the final word; Christ now defines the center from which he lives.'},{heading:'Created Before We Achieved',type:'teaching',content:'Genesis grounds human dignity in God’s creation, not in achievement, popularity, race, wealth, education, health, or social approval. Identity begins as gift before it becomes responsibility.'},{heading:'Culture Matters but Is Not Lord',type:'worldview',content:'Language, family history, race, nationality, neighborhood, traditions, and experiences genuinely shape people. Christianity does not require pretending those differences are meaningless. It does require that no cultural identity become more authoritative than Jesus.'},{heading:'Christ Challenges Both Shame and Pride',type:'christ',content:'Some labels tell us we are less valuable; others tempt us to think we are superior. The gospel confronts both. At the cross, no one earns grace, and in Christ no believer can boast over another.'},{heading:'A New Community Learns a New Way to See',type:'application',content:'Colossians 3 describes a community where old hostilities and status systems are no longer permitted to define relationships. Unity in Christ is not sameness; it is a new allegiance strong enough to reshape how different people treat one another.'},{heading:'Live It This Week',type:'challenge',content:'Notice one label you use to feel superior and one label you use to feel ashamed. Bring both to Christ. Then intentionally honor someone whose background, personality, generation, or social identity differs from yours.'}],questions:['What changes when Paul says Christ lives in him?','Which identities are gifts, and which become idols when made ultimate?','Does identity in Christ erase race, culture, family, or personality? Why not?','How can a positive label become a source of pride or fear?','What does Christian unity require without demanding sameness?','Where do you need Christ to reorder the way you see yourself or someone else?'],prayer:'Jesus, thank You for knowing us fully and calling us to belong to You. Free us from shame, pride, comparison, and labels that compete with Your lordship. Help us honor the real stories that shape people while finding our deepest identity in Your grace. Amen.',background:[{heading:'Galatians and belonging',content:'Galatians addresses whether Gentile believers must adopt Jewish boundary markers to belong fully to God’s people. Paul insists that belonging is grounded in Christ rather than ethnic or religious status.'},{heading:'Colossians and the new humanity',content:'Colossians 3 joins renewed identity with changed relationships. The new self is expressed in concrete community practices, not merely private self-understanding.'},{heading:'Image of God',content:'Genesis places dignity before social ranking. Every human being bears God-given worth before any later label is applied.'}],theology:['Creation: identity begins with being created by God.','Sin: people turn good identities into idols, weapons, or sources of shame.','Union with Christ: believers participate in Christ’s life and lordship.','Grace: belonging is received, not achieved.','Church: the gospel creates a community where difference is reconciled rather than weaponized.'],worldview:{foundation:'God creates people with dignity and calls believers into a new primary allegiance to Christ.',affirm:'Culture, family, race, language, vocation, and community can carry real meaning and gifts.',caution:'Any identity can become idolatrous when it determines worth, truth, loyalty, or neighbor-love more than Jesus does.',response:'Receive your story with gratitude, reject superiority and shame, and submit every identity to the character and commands of Christ.'},difficultQuestions:[{question:'Does “identity in Christ” mean cultural identity should not matter?',response:'No. Scripture recognizes peoples, languages, families, and nations. The issue is ultimacy. Christ does not require cultural amnesia, but He does judge every culture and creates a deeper unity across difference.'},{question:'Is it wrong to feel proud of family or heritage?',response:'Gratitude for heritage can be healthy. Pride becomes sinful when gratitude turns into superiority, contempt, exclusion, or refusal to let Christ correct our group.'},{question:'What if a label describes a real struggle or condition?',response:'Accurate descriptions can help people seek support and understand experience. The danger is reducing the whole person to one condition, achievement, failure, or social category.'}],misunderstandings:['Identity in Christ requires pretending human differences do not exist.','Every cultural tradition is equally faithful to Christ.','Having a strong identity means never receiving correction.','A person’s worst mistake is their permanent identity.'],ministryApplications:{personal:['Notice which labels control your mood or self-worth.','Practice introducing yourself to God in prayer without achievements or roles.'],family:['Tell family stories with gratitude without teaching superiority.','Affirm character and faithfulness more than image or status.'],church:['Make room for different cultures and generations without tokenizing people.','Confront jokes or habits that reduce people to stereotypes.'],community:['Listen to someone whose experience differs from yours.','Refuse language that treats a group label as the whole person.']}});

A({week:4,title:'Living as Ambassadors of Christ',summary:'Christians represent Jesus in ordinary public life. Our message of reconciliation must be visible in the way we speak, work, disagree, serve, and repair harm.',scripture:['2 Corinthians 5:14–21','Matthew 5:13–16','1 Peter 3:15–16'],memoryVerse:'2 Corinthians 5:20',bigIdea:'An ambassador does not represent personal ego or party loyalty first. Believers are entrusted with Christ’s message of reconciliation and are called to make His character credible through truthful, humble, courageous lives.',objectives:['Explain Paul’s picture of Christians as ambassadors.','Connect reconciliation with both message and behavior.','Identify ways public conduct can strengthen or weaken witness.','Practice conviction with gentleness and respect.','Choose one relationship or setting where Christ can be represented more faithfully.'],openingQuestion:'If someone formed their opinion of Jesus only from watching your public behavior for one week, what might they conclude?',sections:[{heading:'Reconciliation Comes Before Representation',type:'scripture',content:'Read 2 Corinthians 5:14–21. Paul begins with what God has done in Christ: God reconciles and then entrusts reconciled people with a ministry of reconciliation. We represent a grace we first received.'},{heading:'Ambassadors Speak for Another',type:'teaching',content:'An ambassador does not have unlimited freedom to invent the message. Christians belong to Christ and therefore cannot treat personal preferences, anger, status, or partisan identity as if they were the gospel.'},{heading:'Character Carries the Message',type:'application',content:'Truthfulness, reliability, patience, sexual integrity, generosity, humility, and the way we treat people with less power all communicate something about the Lord we claim to represent.'},{heading:'Gentleness Is Not Weak Conviction',type:'discernment',content:'First Peter 3 joins a clear reason for hope with gentleness and respect. Christian courage is not measured by how harshly we can speak. We can hold firm convictions without humiliating people.'},{heading:'Light Serves Rather Than Performs',type:'christ',content:'Jesus calls disciples salt and light so that good works point beyond the disciple to the Father. Witness is not personal branding. It is a life that makes God’s goodness visible.'},{heading:'Live It This Week',type:'challenge',content:'Choose one place where your witness is regularly tested: home, work, school, social media, church, or community. Before entering that space, pray: “Jesus, help me represent You rather than merely react as myself.”'}],questions:['What has God done before He calls us ambassadors?','What is the difference between representing Christ and representing a Christian subculture?','Which behaviors make the gospel harder for others to hear?','How can gentleness strengthen rather than weaken conviction?','Where are Christians tempted to confuse partisan loyalty with Christ’s mission?','What repair might be needed where your witness has been inconsistent?'],prayer:'Lord Jesus, thank You for reconciling us to God. Make our words, work, reactions, and relationships worthy of the message we carry. Give us courage without arrogance, gentleness without compromise, and integrity when no one is applauding. Amen.',background:[{heading:'Paul’s ministry of reconciliation',content:'Second Corinthians defends a cruciform ministry shaped by Christ’s self-giving love rather than status and self-promotion.'},{heading:'Ambassador language',content:'An ambassador represents the interests and message of the one who sends them. Paul uses the image to describe believers carrying God’s appeal of reconciliation.'},{heading:'Public witness in 1 Peter',content:'First Peter addresses Christians living as a minority community. Their defense of hope is joined to honorable conduct, gentleness, and respect.'}],theology:['Reconciliation: God acts first in Christ to restore relationship.','Mission: reconciled people are sent with a message and way of life.','Holiness: public witness includes transformed character.','Incarnation: Christian witness is embodied, not merely stated.','Humility: the messenger is not the center of the message.'],worldview:{foundation:'Christians belong to Jesus and represent His kingdom in every sphere of life.',affirm:'Public participation, civic engagement, work, art, education, service, and advocacy can all become arenas of faithful witness.',caution:'When Christian identity is fused with ego, tribe, nationalism, celebrity, or party, the gospel can be distorted.',response:'Speak truth, serve people, pursue reconciliation, and let conduct make the message credible.'},difficultQuestions:[{question:'Does being an ambassador mean Christians must always be nice?',response:'Christian love includes courage, boundaries, correction, and sometimes confrontation. The issue is whether our manner reflects truth, dignity, self-control, and the goal of reconciliation rather than contempt.'},{question:'Can Christians be politically active?',response:'Yes, but political participation remains under Christ’s lordship. No party or candidate should be treated as the kingdom of God, and Christians must be willing to critique their own side.'},{question:'What if I have already damaged my witness?',response:'Confession, apology, restitution where possible, and changed behavior can themselves become a truthful witness to grace.'}],misunderstandings:['Witness is only verbal evangelism.','Strong conviction requires aggressive communication.','Christians must defend every action of other Christians.','Public success is the same as kingdom faithfulness.'],ministryApplications:{personal:['Review one recent conflict through the question: Did I represent Christ?','Apologize where your manner contradicted your message.'],family:['Practice disagreement without contempt at home.','Let children see adults admit wrong and make repairs.'],church:['Train leaders to represent Christ in public communication and online spaces.','Evaluate ministry success by fruit, not visibility alone.'],community:['Serve someone with no expectation of recognition.','Build a relationship across a social or ideological divide.']}});

A({week:5,title:'Faith Over Fear',summary:'Biblical faith does not pretend danger, anxiety, grief, or uncertainty are unreal. It anchors us in God’s presence so fear does not become our master.',scripture:['Isaiah 41:8–10','Mark 4:35–41','Philippians 4:4–9','Psalm 56:3–4'],memoryVerse:'Isaiah 41:10',bigIdea:'Faith over fear is not emotional denial. It is learning to bring fear into God’s presence, receive His strength, take wise action, and refuse to let anxiety decide our obedience.',objectives:['Distinguish courage from pretending not to feel fear.','Identify God’s promises in Isaiah 41.','See how Jesus meets frightened disciples in Mark 4.','Practice prayer and wise thought patterns from Philippians 4.','Choose one faithful action fear has been delaying.'],openingQuestion:'What is the difference between feeling afraid and allowing fear to make the decision?',sections:[{heading:'God’s Command Rests on God’s Presence',type:'scripture',content:'Read Isaiah 41:8–10. The repeated reason not to fear is not that nothing difficult will happen. God says, “I am with you,” “I will strengthen you,” “I will help you,” and “I will uphold you.” Courage grows from who is present.'},{heading:'Fear Is a Human Response, Not Automatic Failure',type:'clarity',content:'Scripture is full of faithful people who experienced fear, distress, grief, and anxiety. The issue is not whether the body ever feels alarm. The disciples themselves panic in a storm while Jesus patiently reveals who He is.'},{heading:'Faith Does Not Reject Wise Action',type:'discernment',content:'Trusting God can include making a plan, seeking medical or professional help, leaving danger, asking for counsel, setting boundaries, or preparing responsibly. Recklessness is not stronger faith.'},{heading:'Jesus Is Present in the Storm',type:'christ',content:'In Mark 4 Jesus does not merely calm water; He confronts the disciples with the question of who He is. The story moves from fear of the storm to awe before Jesus. Christian peace is ultimately relational before it is circumstantial.'},{heading:'Train the Mind Toward What Is True',type:'application',content:'Philippians 4 joins prayer, thanksgiving, disciplined thought, and practiced obedience. Fear often tells stories about what might happen. Faith learns to distinguish possibility from certainty and brings imagined futures under God’s care.'},{heading:'Live It This Week',type:'challenge',content:'Name one fear honestly. Write what is known, what is unknown, what responsible action is yours, and what must be entrusted to God. Then take one faithful step you have been avoiding.'}],questions:['What reasons does God give Israel not to fear?','Why is “never feel afraid” an unrealistic definition of faith?','How can prayer and practical action work together?','What does the storm reveal about the disciples and about Jesus?','Which thoughts tend to multiply your fear without evidence?','What faithful action is fear currently delaying?'],prayer:'God, You know what frightens us. Thank You that Your presence is deeper than our uncertainty. Give us wisdom to act where action is needed, humility to ask for help, and courage to trust You with what we cannot control. Amen.',background:[{heading:'Isaiah’s comfort',content:'Isaiah 40–55 speaks hope to God’s people amid displacement and imperial power. “Do not fear” is grounded in God’s covenant faithfulness, not in easy circumstances.'},{heading:'Storm narratives',content:'The Sea of Galilee could become dangerous quickly. Mark uses the disciples’ fear to reveal their growing question about Jesus’ identity.'},{heading:'Philippians from confinement',content:'Paul writes about peace and disciplined thought while living under real constraints. His teaching is not detached from hardship.'}],theology:['Providence: God remains present and active in uncertain circumstances.','Trust: faith rests in God’s character rather than control.','Embodiment: emotional and physical responses are part of human life.','Wisdom: faithful action and trust belong together.','Community: fear is often carried better with others than alone.'],worldview:{foundation:'Security is ultimately found in God rather than complete control of circumstances.',affirm:'Planning, counseling, medicine, safety practices, and supportive relationships can be gifts of wisdom and care.',caution:'Fear can become manipulative when leaders use threats, panic, or imagined catastrophe to control people.',response:'Tell the truth about danger, reject panic and denial, take wise action, and entrust the future to God.'},difficultQuestions:[{question:'Does anxiety mean I lack faith?',response:'Not necessarily. Anxiety can involve temperament, trauma, stress, medical conditions, circumstances, or learned patterns. Spiritual practices can help, and professional care may also be appropriate. Shame is not a treatment plan.'},{question:'Should Christians ever prepare for bad outcomes?',response:'Yes. Scripture commends wisdom and prudent preparation. Preparation becomes unhealthy when it turns into obsession, hoarding, exploitation, or a substitute for trust.'},{question:'Why does God say not to fear if bad things still happen?',response:'The promise is not that believers avoid every painful event. The deeper promise is God’s presence, sustaining grace, ultimate redemption, and the freedom to obey without fear becoming lord.'}],misunderstandings:['Faith means never experiencing anxiety or fear.','Prayer replaces medical, professional, or practical help.','Courage means staying in unsafe situations.','Every frightening possibility deserves equal attention.'],ministryApplications:{personal:['Limit repeated exposure to fear-driven media when it stops informing and starts controlling you.','Name fears in prayer rather than hiding them.'],family:['Talk about danger without catastrophizing.','Create simple plans that increase safety without feeding panic.'],church:['Avoid using end-times fear or crisis language to manipulate people.','Know referral pathways for counseling and crisis support.'],community:['Be a calm, truthful presence during emergencies.','Help vulnerable neighbors prepare for practical needs.']}});

A({week:6,title:'Engaging Culture Without Compromise',summary:'Jesus sends His people into the world without asking them to become identical to it. Faithful engagement requires presence, discernment, love, and a renewed mind.',scripture:['Romans 12:1–2','John 17:14–18','Matthew 5:13–16','1 Corinthians 9:19–23'],memoryVerse:'Romans 12:2',bigIdea:'Christians are neither called to withdraw from the world nor absorb every value around them. We live present and available to our neighbors while allowing Jesus to transform our minds and practices.',objectives:['Explain the difference between isolation, assimilation, and faithful engagement.','Identify cultural values that can be affirmed and values that require resistance.','Practice discernment without contempt for people.','Connect mission with presence and holiness.','Choose one setting where you can engage more faithfully.'],openingQuestion:'When Christians disagree with something in culture, is our first instinct usually to withdraw, attack, blend in, or understand?',sections:[{heading:'Transformation Before Reaction',type:'scripture',content:'Romans 12 begins by calling believers to offer their whole lives to God and to be transformed by renewed minds. Cultural discernment is not mainly about spotting what is wrong “out there.” It begins with God reshaping us.'},{heading:'Jesus Sends Us Into the World',type:'christ',content:'In John 17 Jesus does not ask the Father to remove His disciples from the world. He asks that they be kept faithful while being sent into it. Christian mission requires real presence among people.'},{heading:'Culture Contains Gifts and Distortions',type:'worldview',content:'Every culture reflects human creativity, relationships, language, art, work, and longing. Every culture is also touched by sin. Discernment asks what can be received with gratitude, what can be redirected, and what must be resisted.'},{heading:'Adaptation Is Not the Same as Compromise',type:'discernment',content:'Paul adapts his approach in 1 Corinthians 9 for the sake of reaching people, but his allegiance to Christ remains fixed. Methods can change while the gospel and Christian character remain nonnegotiable.'},{heading:'Salt and Light Require Proximity',type:'application',content:'Salt that never touches food and light that remains hidden accomplish little. Christians influence culture through relationships, excellent work, truthful speech, service, creativity, hospitality, and courage—not only denunciation.'},{heading:'Live It This Week',type:'challenge',content:'Choose one cultural practice, trend, or community you usually react to quickly. Ask three questions: What is good here? What is broken here? What would faithful presence look like instead of withdrawal or imitation?'}],questions:['What does Romans 12 say must happen before discernment?','Why does Jesus send disciples into the world rather than remove them?','Can culture contain both beauty and sin at the same time?','What is the difference between changing methods and changing convictions?','Where has Christian isolation weakened witness?','Where are you tempted either to blend in or to withdraw?'],prayer:'Father, renew our minds. Keep us from fear, pride, imitation, and isolation. Teach us to recognize what is good, resist what is destructive, and remain lovingly present with people You call us to serve. Make us salt and light that point to Jesus. Amen.',background:[{heading:'Romans and worship',content:'Romans 12 follows eleven chapters describing God’s mercy. Cultural nonconformity is a response to grace and an act of whole-life worship.'},{heading:'John 17 and mission',content:'Jesus frames holiness and mission together: disciples belong to Him, are kept in truth, and are sent into the world.'},{heading:'Paul’s adaptability',content:'First Corinthians 9 shows missionary flexibility without moral relativism. Paul changes approach for the sake of others while remaining under Christ’s law.'}],theology:['Holiness: God forms a distinct people in character and love.','Mission: holiness is not withdrawal but faithful presence.','Common grace: truth and beauty can appear outside the church.','Sin: every culture, including church culture, needs correction.','Wisdom: application requires discernment rather than a single rule for every situation.'],worldview:{foundation:'Jesus is Lord over every culture, including the culture we personally prefer.',affirm:'Art, science, work, family, community, technology, and civic life can reflect human creativity and common good.',caution:'Christians can confuse nostalgia, nationalism, class habits, or church subculture with biblical faithfulness.',response:'Listen carefully, test everything, keep what is good, resist what contradicts Christ, and remain present in love.'},difficultQuestions:[{question:'Should Christians boycott things they believe are harmful?',response:'Sometimes withdrawal can be a faithful boundary or form of witness. Other times presence and engagement may do more good. Ask what the action communicates, who it affects, whether alternatives exist, and what love and wisdom require.'},{question:'Does engagement mean affirming everything about people?',response:'No. Genuine relationship can include disagreement. Respecting dignity and listening carefully do not require abandoning conviction.'},{question:'How do I know when adaptation becomes compromise?',response:'Ask whether the change alters the gospel, violates a clear biblical command, damages character, requires dishonesty, or treats people as tools rather than neighbors.'}],misunderstandings:['Everything old is biblical and everything new is compromise.','Engagement requires agreement.','Holiness means avoiding relationships with people who believe differently.','Cultural influence is measured mainly by political power.'],ministryApplications:{personal:['Audit which media and communities most shape your imagination.','Practice curiosity before criticism.'],family:['Discuss cultural trends without mockery or panic.','Teach children how to evaluate rather than merely fear culture.'],church:['Create spaces where questions can be asked honestly.','Serve the community beyond church membership.'],community:['Participate in local institutions with integrity and humility.','Build genuine relationships before trying to win arguments.']}});

A({week:7,title:'Racial Reconciliation',summary:'Jesus tears down hostility and creates one people without erasing the real histories and differences people bring. Reconciliation requires truth, repentance, justice, and shared life.',scripture:['Ephesians 2:11–22','Acts 10:34–35','Revelation 7:9–10','Galatians 3:26–29'],memoryVerse:'Ephesians 2:14',bigIdea:'Biblical reconciliation is more than polite coexistence. In Christ, hostility loses its right to rule, dignity is shared, truth is faced, and divided people learn to become family.',objectives:['Read Ephesians 2 in its Jewish-Gentile context.','Distinguish unity from sameness or avoidance.','Recognize prejudice as a discipleship issue.','Connect reconciliation with truth, repentance, justice, and relationship.','Take one concrete bridge-building step.'],openingQuestion:'Can people say “we are all one in Christ” while still avoiding the wounds, inequalities, or prejudices between them?',sections:[{heading:'Begin with a Real Ethnic Divide',type:'scripture',content:'Ephesians 2 addresses hostility between Jews and Gentiles, communities divided by history, religion, identity, and social boundaries. Paul does not pretend those differences never existed. He announces that Christ has made peace and created a new humanity.'},{heading:'Reconciliation Is Accomplished by Jesus',type:'christ',content:'Christ is our peace. The cross does not merely give private forgiveness; it creates a reconciled people. Christian unity therefore rests in Jesus before it rests in shared preference, politics, music, or culture.'},{heading:'Unity Does Not Require Erasing Difference',type:'clarity',content:'Revelation pictures a redeemed multitude from every nation, tribe, people, and language. Difference remains visible while worship is united. Biblical reconciliation is not colorblindness or cultural sameness.'},{heading:'Prejudice Distorts How We See Image-Bearers',type:'discernment',content:'Racial prejudice assigns character, danger, worth, intelligence, or moral status based on group identity. It can operate personally and through habits or systems. Christians are called to examine both hearts and practices.'},{heading:'Truth and Justice Strengthen Reconciliation',type:'application',content:'Forgiveness does not require pretending harm never happened. Reconciliation may involve listening, confession, restitution, changed policies, patient relationship, and protection from ongoing harm. Peace built on silence is fragile.'},{heading:'Live It This Week',type:'challenge',content:'Listen to the experience of a Christian from a different racial or cultural background without immediately explaining, defending, or comparing. Ask what faithful solidarity and friendship might look like in your actual community.'}],questions:['What barriers separated Jews and Gentiles in Ephesians 2?','What does it mean that Christ “is our peace”?','Why is unity different from sameness?','Can reconciliation happen without truth about harm?','How can churches unintentionally preserve racial separation?','What bridge can you help build without centering yourself?'],prayer:'Jesus, You are our peace. Expose prejudice, pride, fear, and indifference in us. Give us courage to hear truth, repent where needed, pursue justice, and build relationships across barriers. Make Your church a credible picture of the reconciled family You are creating. Amen.',background:[{heading:'Jew and Gentile',content:'The New Testament church wrestled deeply with ethnic and covenant boundaries. Ephesians 2 describes Gentile exclusion and Christ’s work of creating shared citizenship and household belonging.'},{heading:'Peter and Cornelius',content:'Acts 10 records God confronting Peter’s inherited boundary assumptions and declaring that God shows no partiality.'},{heading:'Revelation’s multitude',content:'The final vision is not cultural erasure but a worshiping multitude whose nations and languages remain named.'}],theology:['Image of God: racial hierarchy contradicts shared human dignity.','Reconciliation: Christ creates peace across hostile divisions.','Sin: prejudice can be personal, communal, and institutional.','Repentance: reconciliation requires changed thinking and practice.','Eschatology: the church anticipates God’s multiethnic redeemed people.'],worldview:{foundation:'No race possesses greater God-given dignity, and no racial group can claim privileged access to grace.',affirm:'Cultural heritage, language, history, and distinct communal traditions can be gifts worth honoring.',caution:'Calls for unity can be used to silence truthful accounts of injustice, while justice language can also be used in ways that dehumanize opponents.',response:'Tell the truth, reject superiority, listen, repent, repair where possible, pursue justice, and build durable relationships in Christ.'},difficultQuestions:[{question:'Should Christians be “colorblind”?',response:'The intention may be to reject prejudice, but Scripture’s vision is richer: equal dignity and unity without pretending cultural and historical differences are invisible.'},{question:'Is racism only an individual attitude?',response:'Personal prejudice matters, but laws, institutions, customs, economic patterns, and church practices can also produce unequal treatment. Christians should examine both hearts and structures with evidence and humility.'},{question:'Does reconciliation mean immediate trust?',response:'No. Forgiveness, reconciliation, and restored trust are related but distinct. Trust may require time, accountability, safety, and demonstrated change.'}],misunderstandings:['Talking about race always creates division.','Unity means everyone must stop discussing historical harm.','Only intentional hatred can produce racial injustice.','Reconciliation requires the harmed person to ignore boundaries or safety.'],ministryApplications:{personal:['Examine stereotypes you repeat or assume.','Learn the history of racial relationships in your local community.'],family:['Challenge racial jokes or stereotypes instead of normalizing them.','Expose children to faithful voices from different cultures.'],church:['Review leadership, worship, outreach, and decision-making for unnecessary barriers.','Build reciprocal partnerships rather than one-sided charity.'],community:['Support truthful local history and fair treatment.','Choose relationship-building actions that continue after special events end.']}});

A({week:8,title:'Justice and Mercy',summary:'Micah 6:8 refuses the false choice between justice and mercy. God calls His people to do what is right, love steadfast mercy, and walk humbly without turning righteousness into revenge.',scripture:['Micah 6:6–8','Amos 5:21–24','Matthew 23:23','Luke 10:25–37'],memoryVerse:'Micah 6:8',bigIdea:'Biblical justice protects dignity and confronts wrong; mercy seeks restoration and compassion; humility remembers that we remain accountable to God while pursuing both.',objectives:['Read Micah 6:8 in its covenant setting.','Distinguish justice from revenge and mercy from permissiveness.','See justice and mercy together in Jesus’ teaching.','Identify ways worship can become detached from neighbor-love.','Practice one just, merciful, and humble response.'],openingQuestion:'Why do people often act as though justice and mercy are opposites?',sections:[{heading:'God Is Not Impressed by Religious Performance Alone',type:'scripture',content:'Micah 6 imagines increasingly dramatic offerings, then brings the issue back to a life aligned with God: do justice, love mercy, and walk humbly. Worship that never reaches relationships and public life is incomplete.'},{heading:'Justice Seeks What Is Right',type:'teaching',content:'Biblical justice includes fair treatment, truthful judgment, protection of the vulnerable, accountability for harm, and restoration of right relationships. It is more than personal kindness, but it is never permission for cruelty.'},{heading:'Mercy Is Stronger Than Sentiment',type:'christ',content:'Jesus repeatedly joins mercy with truth and responsibility. Mercy does not call evil good. It refuses to reduce a person to their worst act and seeks restoration where restoration is possible.'},{heading:'Revenge Is Not Justice',type:'discernment',content:'Justice asks what is right, proportionate, truthful, protective, and restorative. Revenge asks how to make someone hurt because we hurt. Anger can alert us to wrong, but anger alone is not a trustworthy judge.'},{heading:'Humility Keeps the Pursuer of Justice Accountable',type:'application',content:'Walking humbly means listening, checking facts, acknowledging bias, accepting limits, and remembering that we too need grace. Humility does not weaken justice; it protects justice from becoming self-righteousness.'},{heading:'Live It This Week',type:'challenge',content:'Choose one conflict or public issue. Ask: What would be just? Who is vulnerable? Where is mercy needed? What facts do I still need? What would humility require from me before I speak or act?'}],questions:['What problem is Micah addressing beyond religious ritual?','How is justice different from revenge?','Can mercy include consequences?','Why does Jesus criticize people who tithe carefully but neglect justice and mercy?','How can humility improve rather than weaken advocacy?','Where do you personally lean toward justice without mercy or mercy without justice?'],prayer:'God of justice and mercy, correct our distorted loves. Give us courage to confront wrong, compassion toward people, wisdom about consequences, and humility about ourselves. Make our worship visible in the way we protect, restore, forgive, and act. Amen.',background:[{heading:'Micah’s covenant lawsuit',content:'Micah addresses a society marked by exploitation and corrupt leadership. Chapter 6 uses courtroom imagery to expose a disconnect between religious observance and covenant faithfulness.'},{heading:'Amos and worship',content:'Amos 5 similarly rejects worship separated from justice. God’s concern for public righteousness is not a modern addition to biblical faith.'},{heading:'Jesus and the weightier matters',content:'Matthew 23:23 names justice, mercy, and faithfulness as weightier matters while criticizing meticulous religion that neglects them.'}],theology:['Justice: God’s character establishes what is right and fair.','Mercy: God’s steadfast love seeks restoration.','Sin: power can be used to exploit and self-justify.','Holiness: worship and ethics cannot be separated.','Humility: righteous action remains accountable to God and truth.'],worldview:{foundation:'God cares about both personal morality and the social conditions that affect neighbors.',affirm:'Law, advocacy, charity, restitution, public policy, peacemaking, and institutional reform can all be tools for justice when used wisely.',caution:'Justice language can be reduced to revenge, partisan slogans, selective outrage, or certainty without evidence.',response:'Seek facts, protect the vulnerable, pursue fair accountability, make room for mercy, and remain humble before God.'},difficultQuestions:[{question:'Does mercy mean someone should avoid consequences?',response:'No. Mercy can coexist with accountability, boundaries, restitution, and legal consequences. The question is whether consequences are truthful, proportionate, protective, and oriented toward what is right rather than vengeance.'},{question:'Is justice only the government’s responsibility?',response:'Government has unique responsibilities, but Scripture calls individuals, families, churches, employers, courts, and communities to fair treatment and protection of vulnerable people.'},{question:'Can Christians disagree about which policy is most just?',response:'Yes. Shared biblical principles do not automatically produce identical policy judgments. Christians should distinguish moral goals from prudential strategies and debate evidence without questioning one another’s salvation.'}],misunderstandings:['Justice and mercy cancel each other out.','Feeling angry proves my preferred response is just.','Humility means staying silent about wrongdoing.','Private charity is the only biblical response to structural injustice.'],ministryApplications:{personal:['Repair one unfair action where you have power to do so.','Check a claim before joining public outrage.'],family:['Teach consequences alongside restoration.','Avoid favoritism between children or relatives.'],church:['Use transparent processes for money, discipline, and complaints.','Care for harmed people rather than protecting institutional reputation.'],community:['Support fair access and due process.','Serve people affected by injustice while also addressing causes where possible.']}});

A({week:9,title:'The Sanctity of Life',summary:'Every human life carries God-given dignity from beginning to end. A consistent Christian ethic of life asks how we protect the vulnerable wherever human worth is threatened.',scripture:['Psalm 139:13–18','Genesis 1:26–27','James 3:9–10','Matthew 25:31–40'],memoryVerse:'Psalm 139:14',bigIdea:'Human worth is received from God rather than earned by age, ability, independence, innocence, productivity, race, health, wealth, or social usefulness.',objectives:['Ground human dignity in creation rather than usefulness.','Explain why a Christian ethic of life must be consistent across the lifespan.','Recognize vulnerable people whom society can overlook.','Distinguish moral conviction from dehumanizing rhetoric.','Choose one concrete act that protects or honors life.'],openingQuestion:'Which kinds of people does society most easily treat as inconvenient, invisible, burdensome, or disposable?',sections:[{heading:'Life Is Known by God',type:'scripture',content:'Psalm 139 celebrates God’s intimate knowledge of human life. The passage is poetry, not a biology textbook, but its theological claim is powerful: human life is not anonymous to God.'},{heading:'Dignity Begins with the Image of God',type:'teaching',content:'Genesis places human worth before achievement. Dignity therefore does not rise when a person becomes independent or productive, and it does not fall when a person becomes weak, disabled, sick, poor, imprisoned, elderly, or dependent.'},{heading:'A Consistent Ethic of Life',type:'worldview',content:'Protecting life includes concern for unborn children and mothers, children and families, people with disabilities, victims of violence, immigrants, prisoners, people in poverty, the sick, the elderly, and those facing despair. Different issues involve different moral and policy questions, but dignity is not selective.'},{heading:'Words Can Violate the Dignity We Claim to Defend',type:'discernment',content:'James warns that the same tongue can bless God and curse people made in God’s likeness. Christians undermine a pro-life witness when they describe opponents, offenders, migrants, the poor, or any other group as less than human.'},{heading:'Jesus Identifies with the Vulnerable',type:'christ',content:'Matthew 25 places service to hungry, sick, imprisoned, and marginalized people in direct relationship to Jesus. Christian care for life moves beyond slogans into costly presence.'},{heading:'Live It This Week',type:'challenge',content:'Choose one person or group whose dignity is easy for you to overlook. Learn one concrete need, pray by name if possible, and take one action that protects, supports, visits, provides, advocates, or listens.'}],questions:['Where does human dignity come from in Scripture?','Why is productivity a dangerous measure of worth?','What would a “womb to tomb” ethic of life require?','How can dehumanizing language contradict a stated concern for life?','Which vulnerable people did Jesus move toward?','Where is God asking you to move from opinion to practical care?'],prayer:'Creator God, thank You for the dignity You give every human life. Forgive us when our concern becomes selective or our words deny the image of God in others. Give us courage to protect the vulnerable, support families, care for the suffering, and honor life with both conviction and compassion. Amen.',background:[{heading:'Psalm 139 as worship',content:'The psalm praises God’s complete knowledge and presence. Its language about formation in the womb expresses divine care and intentionality.'},{heading:'Image-bearing after the fall',content:'Genesis 9 and James 3 continue to ground human treatment in God’s likeness even after sin enters the story.'},{heading:'Jesus and vulnerable people',content:'The Gospels repeatedly show Jesus welcoming children, touching the sick, honoring women, receiving outsiders, feeding crowds, and restoring people pushed to the margins.'}],theology:['Creation: human life is God’s gift.','Image of God: dignity is inherent rather than earned.','Incarnation: Jesus affirms embodied human life by taking flesh.','Neighbor-love: moral conviction becomes practical care.','Hope: Christian opposition to death is rooted in resurrection.'],worldview:{foundation:'Every human being has God-given dignity that cannot be reduced to usefulness, status, health, innocence, or political value.',affirm:'Medicine, disability advocacy, maternal support, adoption and foster care, violence prevention, prison ministry, elder care, poverty relief, and mental-health support can all express a culture of life.',caution:'A life ethic becomes distorted when it protects only people useful to our political identity or treats complex situations without compassion.',response:'Defend vulnerable life consistently, support people facing hard circumstances, speak with dignity, and accept personal cost when love requires it.'},difficultQuestions:[{question:'Can Christians agree on the value of life but disagree about policy?',response:'Yes. Some moral convictions are clear while policy involves questions of law, effectiveness, tradeoffs, jurisdiction, and unintended consequences. Debate those questions without abandoning the shared dignity of people affected.'},{question:'Does valuing life require preserving biological life by every medical means?',response:'Christian ethics distinguishes ordinary care from burdensome or futile interventions. End-of-life decisions require wisdom, medical facts, patient wishes, family care, and pastoral support; refusing extraordinary treatment is not automatically the same as intending death.'},{question:'How should the church respond to someone facing an unexpected pregnancy?',response:'With truth, practical support, confidentiality within safeguarding limits, material help, healthcare connection, non-shaming presence, and long-term community rather than slogans alone.'}],misunderstandings:['Human worth depends on innocence or productivity.','A consistent ethic of life means every policy question has one obvious Christian answer.','Strong moral conviction excuses demeaning language.','Caring for life ends once a crisis moment passes.'],ministryApplications:{personal:['Replace dehumanizing language with truthful, specific language.','Support one vulnerable person in a practical way.'],family:['Discuss dignity across disability, age, illness, and dependence.','Prepare to support relatives through difficult care seasons.'],church:['Build partnerships for pregnancy support, foster care, disability inclusion, mental health, prison ministry, and elder care.','Make accessibility and safeguarding part of ministry planning.'],community:['Support services that reduce preventable harm and strengthen families.','Advocate for policies while treating opponents and affected people with dignity.']}});

A({week:10,title:'Immigration and the Stranger',summary:'Scripture repeatedly commands God’s people to remember the vulnerable outsider. Christians can debate immigration policy while refusing fear, contempt, exploitation, or indifference toward immigrants and refugees.',scripture:['Leviticus 19:33–34','Deuteronomy 10:17–19','Matthew 25:35','Hebrews 13:1–3'],memoryVerse:'Leviticus 19:34',bigIdea:'Biblical hospitality does not erase questions of law, borders, security, or policy. It does establish a nonnegotiable moral floor: the foreigner is a neighbor bearing God’s image and must not be mistreated or dehumanized.',objectives:['Understand Israel’s command to remember its own experience as strangers.','Distinguish biblical moral principles from specific modern policy proposals.','Reject dehumanizing language toward immigrants and refugees.','Hold compassion, justice, law, and wisdom together.','Take one practical step of hospitality or service.'],openingQuestion:'Can Christians disagree about border policy while still agreeing on how an immigrant should be treated as a human being?',sections:[{heading:'Remember That You Were Strangers',type:'scripture',content:'Leviticus and Deuteronomy ground treatment of foreigners in memory. Israel is told not to reproduce the oppression it experienced in Egypt. Remembered suffering should create empathy and justice rather than fear.'},{heading:'The Stranger Bears God-Given Dignity',type:'teaching',content:'Immigration status does not determine human worth. Citizens, undocumented migrants, refugees, asylum seekers, temporary workers, and people waiting through legal processes all remain people made in God’s image.'},{heading:'Biblical Commands Are Not a Complete Immigration Statute',type:'clarity',content:'Ancient Israel was not a modern nation-state, and biblical “stranger” categories do not map perfectly onto every current legal category. Scripture gives moral principles that should shape policy judgment without pretending it directly answers every administrative question.'},{heading:'Law and Compassion Are Not Enemies',type:'discernment',content:'Governments have responsibilities for order and public safety; Christians also have responsibilities for justice, due process, family integrity, hospitality, and protection from exploitation. Mature discussion refuses to reduce the issue to one slogan.'},{heading:'Jesus Draws Near to the Stranger',type:'christ',content:'Jesus identifies Himself with the stranger welcomed in Matthew 25. The church’s first question cannot only be “What category are you?” It must also ask, “How can we love our neighbor faithfully within the responsibilities we have?”'},{heading:'Live It This Week',type:'challenge',content:'Learn the name and story of someone who immigrated to your community, support a trustworthy ministry or legal-aid organization, help with language or practical needs, or examine the accuracy and tone of the claims you repeat about immigrants.'}],questions:['Why does God connect treatment of foreigners with Israel’s memory of Egypt?','What moral principles are clear even when policy details are disputed?','How can concern for law be expressed without contempt?','How can compassion be practiced without pretending difficult policy questions do not exist?','What forms of exploitation are immigrants especially vulnerable to?','What would neighbor-love look like in your local community?'],prayer:'God who loves the stranger, give us wisdom beyond slogans. Protect us from fear, indifference, and dehumanizing speech. Help leaders pursue justice and order, help churches practice hospitality, and help us see every immigrant and refugee first as a person made in Your image. Amen.',background:[{heading:'The ger in Israel',content:'The Old Testament uses several terms for outsiders. The resident foreigner often lacked land and kinship protections and therefore received special legal and ethical concern.'},{heading:'Memory of Egypt',content:'Israel’s own history of oppression becomes a repeated moral reason for protecting vulnerable outsiders.'},{heading:'New Testament hospitality',content:'The church continues a strong ethic of hospitality, care for strangers, and welcome across ethnic boundaries even though it does not function as a nation-state.'}],theology:['Image of God: immigration status does not determine dignity.','Hospitality: God’s people make room for vulnerable outsiders.','Justice: law should protect rather than exploit the weak.','Government: public authorities have real responsibilities for order and protection.','Mission: migration often creates opportunities for mutual service and gospel relationship.'],worldview:{foundation:'The foreigner is a neighbor with God-given dignity; policy must never become permission for dehumanization.',affirm:'Nations may maintain laws, borders, identity processes, and security practices when pursued justly and humanely.',caution:'Fear-based rhetoric can exaggerate threats, while simplistic compassion can ignore legitimate questions of capacity, law, trafficking, labor exploitation, and public order.',response:'Seek accurate facts, humane law, due process, family care, protection from exploitation, and practical hospitality.'},difficultQuestions:[{question:'Does the Bible command open borders?',response:'The Bible does not present a modern border-policy blueprint. It clearly commands justice and humane treatment of foreigners. Christians must reason from those principles alongside contemporary legal and practical realities.'},{question:'Is entering or remaining without legal status automatically a complete moral judgment of the person?',response:'Legal violations matter, but people’s situations can involve asylum law, trafficking, family circumstances, administrative delay, changing status, or other complexity. A legal category should not become a total moral label.'},{question:'Can a Christian support stricter enforcement?',response:'Christians may disagree about enforcement policy. Any approach should still respect human dignity, proportionality, due process, family impact, truthfulness, and protection from abuse.'}],misunderstandings:['Every biblical reference to a stranger maps directly onto modern immigration law.','Compassion requires ignoring law or security.','Concern for law permits dehumanizing people.','All immigrants have the same legal status, story, or needs.'],ministryApplications:{personal:['Verify statistics and stories before repeating them.','Learn basic respectful language for the legal categories you discuss.'],family:['Teach children hospitality toward newcomers.','Avoid jokes or stereotypes about accents, nationality, or status.'],church:['Partner with trustworthy immigrant, refugee, language, or legal-aid ministries.','Make worship and practical assistance accessible to newcomers where possible.'],community:['Support anti-trafficking and fair-labor protections.','Advocate for humane, workable processes while listening to communities directly affected.']}});
})();
